#!/usr/bin/env python3
from __future__ import annotations

"""
Cheap evidence metrics and reference-neighbourhood analysis.

Overview
--------
This module implements the evidence-generation stage of the Cheap Context First
structural-context pipeline. It consumes a query material profile together with
candidate pools and derives inexpensive, explainable evidence describing how
the query sits relative to its reference neighbourhood.

The module deliberately separates evidence generation from final role
assignment. It produces descriptive metrics, evidence concepts, pool-context
summaries, and lightweight role signals that downstream modules can interpret,
but it does not itself make the final Hub / Boundary / Other decision.

Primary inputs
--------------
* ``query_profile.json`` generated by ``material_profile_builder.py``.
* Candidate pool CSV files generated by ``candidate_pool_builder.py``.
* ``candidate_pool_summary.json`` when present.

Primary outputs
---------------
* ``cheap_evidence_summary.json``
* ``cheap_evidence_metrics.csv``
* ``pool_level_metrics.csv``
* ``pool_context_summary.json``
* ``pool_context_summary.csv``
* ``reference_neighbourhood_fingerprint.json``
* ``missing_value_report.csv``
* ``evidence_report.md``

Architectural role
------------------
The module sits between candidate-pool construction and role-prior inference:

    material_profile_builder
            |
            v
    candidate_pool_builder
            |
            v
    cheap_evidence_metrics   <-- this module
            |
            v
    role_prior_engine

Its responsibility is therefore to measure and organise evidence, not to
silently change the meaning of downstream role scores.

Evidence philosophy
-------------------
The implementation favours cheap, deterministic and interpretable evidence:
chemistry overlap, family/prototype agreement, symmetry, stability metadata,
pool diversity, missing-value coverage and neighbourhood structure. Composite
evidence concepts are derived from these existing measurements so that the
pipeline can expose meaningful context without requiring expensive simulation.

Reference-neighbourhood fingerprint
-----------------------------------
The fingerprint includes an ``explainability_summary`` describing dominant
contextual categories, category diversity, pool-level signatures,
classification coverage, resolved space-group labels, narrative summaries and
recurring reference materials.

These explainability additions are descriptive. They do not modify existing
candidate selection, evidence calculations, similarity values or downstream
role scoring. The existing fingerprint schema identifier is retained for
transition compatibility.

Maintenance guidance
--------------------
When extending this module:

* preserve the separation between evidence generation and role assignment;
* prefer deterministic calculations based on existing Phase 1/2 data;
* document new thresholds and derived concepts explicitly;
* treat missing information as missing evidence rather than negative evidence;
* preserve output-schema compatibility where practical;
* avoid introducing expensive structural calculations into this stage.

The file is intentionally explicit rather than compact. Many intermediate
metrics are retained because they support diagnostics, auditability and later
analysis of why a candidate received a particular structural-context prior.
"""


import argparse
